const NIMH='https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/';
const D=`depression|Depression|Mood|A condition marked by persistent low mood or loss of interest lasting at least two weeks and affecting daily life.|Changes in sleep, appetite or energy;Difficulty concentrating;Feelings of worthlessness or hopelessness|Depression is just sadness or weakness.|It is a medical condition with biological, psychological and social contributors. Psychotherapy and medication are established treatments.|depression
bipolar|Bipolar disorder|Mood|Involves episodes of unusually elevated or irritable mood (mania or hypomania), often alternating with depressive episodes.|Episodes differ in length and intensity;Energy and need for sleep shift markedly;Can resemble depression, so careful assessment matters|Bipolar disorder just means mood swings.|Episodes are sustained changes in mood and energy beyond ordinary ups and downs. Treatment usually combines medication and therapy.|bipolar-disorder
gad|Generalized anxiety disorder|Anxiety|Excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters on most days for at least six months, with symptoms such as restlessness and tension.|Feeling on edge or restless;Muscle tension and tiredness;Trouble sleeping or concentrating|GAD is just being a worrier.|The worry is persistent and out of proportion, and it interferes with functioning. CBT and medication are established treatments.|anxiety-disorders
panic|Panic disorder|Anxiety|Recurrent, unexpected panic attacks (sudden surges of intense fear with physical symptoms) plus ongoing worry about having more.|Pounding heart, breathlessness, dizziness;Fear of losing control;Avoiding places where attacks happened|A panic attack means you are dying or going crazy.|Attacks are frightening but not dangerous in themselves. When symptoms first appear, a doctor can rule out physical causes.|anxiety-disorders
phobias|Specific phobias|Anxiety|An intense fear, out of proportion to the danger, of a particular object or situation such as animals, heights or injections.|Immediate fear on exposure or anticipation;Avoidance that limits daily life;Awareness that the fear is excessive|Phobias are just strong dislikes.|They cause real distress and impairment. Exposure-based therapy is a well-supported treatment.|anxiety-disorders
social|Social anxiety disorder|Anxiety|A persistent fear of being judged or embarrassed in social or performance situations, leading to avoidance or intense distress.|Dread before social events;Physical signs such as blushing or shaking;Avoiding school, work or social situations|It is just shyness.|Shyness is a temperament. Social anxiety disorder causes marked distress or impairment and responds to treatment.|anxiety-disorders
ocd|Obsessive-compulsive disorder|OCD & related|Involves obsessions (unwanted intrusive thoughts, images or urges) and compulsions (repetitive behaviours or mental acts) that are distressing or time-consuming.|Obsessions cause anxiety;Compulsions are done to reduce distress;Themes vary widely|OCD is about being neat and tidy.|Cleanliness is only one theme among many. Exposure and response prevention is an established therapy.|obsessive-compulsive-disorder-ocd
ptsd|Post-traumatic stress disorder|Trauma|Can develop after a traumatic event. It involves re-experiencing, avoidance, negative changes in mood and thinking, and heightened arousal lasting beyond a month.|Intrusive memories or flashbacks;Avoiding reminders;Feeling constantly on guard|Only soldiers get PTSD.|It can follow many kinds of trauma, and most people who experience trauma do not develop it. Trauma-focused psychotherapies are established treatments.|post-traumatic-stress-disorder-ptsd
adhd|ADHD|Neurodevelopmental|A neurodevelopmental condition with persistent inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that interferes with functioning and begins in childhood.|Difficulty sustaining attention or organizing tasks;Restlessness or impulsivity;Often continues into adulthood|ADHD is a lack of discipline, or only affects boys.|It is a well-studied condition affecting all genders and is sometimes under-recognised in girls and women.|attention-deficit-hyperactivity-disorder-adhd
autism|Autism spectrum disorder|Neurodevelopmental|A neurodevelopmental condition involving differences in social communication and interaction, plus restricted or repetitive patterns of behaviour or interests. Presentation varies widely.|Differences in social communication;Focused interests and a preference for routine;Sensory differences are common|Autistic people lack empathy.|Many experience and express empathy differently. Support needs vary widely across the spectrum.|autism-spectrum-disorders-asd
anorexia|Anorexia nervosa|Eating|An eating disorder involving restricted eating, intense fear of weight gain and a distorted experience of body weight or shape, with serious medical risks.|Fear of gaining weight;Distorted body image;Serious effects on physical health|Eating disorders are a choice or about vanity.|They are serious mental illnesses with biological, psychological and social factors. Early treatment improves outcomes.|eating-disorders
bulimia|Bulimia nervosa|Eating|Recurrent binge eating followed by compensatory behaviours, with self-worth strongly tied to body shape and weight.|Cycles of bingeing and compensating;Shame and secrecy;People can be of any weight|You can tell by looking at someone.|People of any weight can have an eating disorder, and it needs professional assessment.|eating-disorders
bed|Binge-eating disorder|Eating|Recurrent episodes of eating large amounts with a sense of lost control and distress afterwards, without regular compensatory behaviours.|Feeling out of control while eating;Eating alone or in secret;Guilt or distress afterwards|It is just overeating.|It is a recognised disorder distinct from occasional overeating. Psychotherapy is an established treatment.|eating-disorders
schizophrenia|Schizophrenia|Psychosis|A psychotic disorder that can involve hallucinations, delusions, disorganised thinking and reduced motivation or emotional expression.|Symptoms vary from person to person;Often begins in the late teens to early adulthood;Treatment combines medication, therapy and support|Schizophrenia means a split personality.|It is unrelated to dissociative identity disorder, and most people with schizophrenia are not violent.|schizophrenia
memory|Memory|Everyday|Memory involves encoding, storing and retrieving information, and it is reconstructive rather than a perfect recording.|Working memory holds small amounts briefly;Recalling a memory can alter it;Confidence is not proof of accuracy|Memory works like a video camera.|Research on reconstructive memory shows recall is shaped by later information and expectations.|
attention|Attention|Everyday|Attention is how the mind selects some information to process and ignores the rest, and its capacity is limited.|Selective attention filters input;Fatigue and stress reduce focus;Switching tasks has a cost|People can multitask effectively.|Most attention-demanding tasks suffer when people switch between them.|
learning|Learning|Everyday|Learning is a lasting change in knowledge or behaviour from experience. Some study methods work much better than others.|Spaced practice beats cramming;Testing yourself aids retention;Feedback helps correct errors|People have fixed learning styles, such as visual or auditory.|Reviews have found little good evidence that matching teaching to a preferred style improves learning.|
emotion|Emotion|Everyday|Emotions involve bodily, mental and behavioural components, and people differ in how they notice and manage them.|Emotions carry information about situations;Regulation strategies differ in effectiveness;Naming feelings can help|Suppressing emotions makes them go away.|Habitual suppression is linked with worse outcomes in many studies. Strategies such as reappraisal often work better.|
motivation|Motivation|Everyday|Motivation explains why people start, direct and keep up behaviour, from internal interest to external rewards.|Intrinsic and extrinsic motives differ;Goals and confidence matter;Environment and habits shape action|You must feel motivated before you can act.|Starting a small step often creates motivation rather than waiting for it.|
personality|Personality|Everyday|Personality describes relatively stable patterns of thinking, feeling and behaving that differ between people.|The Big Five is widely used in research;Traits are moderately stable but can change;Traits describe tendencies, not destiny|Personality types like MBTI are scientifically validated.|Research favours trait models such as the Big Five. Type tests show limited ability to predict outcomes.|
attachment|Attachment & relationships|Everyday|Attachment theory proposes that early close relationships shape expectations about closeness and support later in life.|Patterns are described as secure or insecure;They are tendencies, not labels;Experience can shift them|Your attachment style is fixed in childhood.|Patterns are moderately stable but can change with new relationships and experiences.|
habits|Habits|Everyday|Habits are behaviours triggered automatically by cues in familiar contexts after repetition.|Cue, routine and reward;Consistent context builds automaticity;The time needed varies widely|It takes 21 days to form a habit.|Studies find wide individual variation, often taking two months or more.|
decisions|Decision-making|Everyday|Decision-making studies how people weigh options, often using mental shortcuts called heuristics.|Heuristics are fast but can mislead;Fatigue and stress affect choices;Emotion plays a role|Good decisions are purely rational.|Research from Kahneman and Tversky onward shows intuition and emotion shape even careful decisions.|
biases|Cognitive biases|Everyday|Cognitive biases are systematic patterns in how judgement departs from strict logic, such as confirmation bias or anchoring.|Confirmation bias favours agreeing evidence;Anchoring leans on first numbers seen;Awareness helps but does not remove them|Only other people are biased.|People tend to spot bias in others more easily than in themselves.|
sleep|Sleep & dreaming|Everyday|Sleep cycles through stages including REM, and it supports memory, mood and health.|Sleep has distinct stages;Sleep supports memory consolidation;Vivid dreams are most common in REM|You can fully catch up on lost sleep at weekends.|Evidence is mixed, and regular sleep is generally better than long recovery sleeps.|
stress|Stress|Everyday|Stress is the body and mind’s response to demands. How we appraise a situation affects how strongly we respond.|Acute and chronic stress differ;Appraisal shapes the response;Chronic stress is linked to health problems|All stress is harmful.|Short-term stress can be adaptive. Prolonged stress is the main health concern.|`;
const TOPICS=[{id:'anxiety',t:'Anxiety: an overview',c:'Anxiety',s:'Everyday anxiety versus anxiety disorders, what it involves, myths and facts, and where to find help.'}].concat(D.trim().split('\n').map(l=>{const[id,t,c,s,k,m,f,u]=l.split('|');return{id,t,c,s,k:k.split(';'),m,f,u}}));
const EX={memory:['Loftus, E. F., & Palmer, J. C. (1974). Reconstruction of automobile destruction. <em>Journal of Verbal Learning and Verbal Behavior</em>, 13, 585–589.'],attention:['Rubinstein, J. S., Meyer, D. E., & Evans, J. E. (2001). Executive control of cognitive processes in task switching. <em>Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance</em>, 27, 763–797.'],learning:['Pashler, H., McDaniel, M., Rohrer, D., & Bjork, R. (2008). Learning styles: Concepts and evidence. <em>Psychological Science in the Public Interest</em>, 9, 105–119.'],emotion:['Gross, J. J., & John, O. P. (2003). Individual differences in two emotion regulation processes. <em>Journal of Personality and Social Psychology</em>, 85, 348–362.'],motivation:['Ryan, R. M., & Deci, E. L. (2000). Self-determination theory and the facilitation of intrinsic motivation, social development, and well-being. <em>American Psychologist</em>, 55, 68–78.'],habits:['Lally, P., van Jaarsveld, C. H. M., Potts, H. W. W., & Wardle, J. (2010). How are habits formed: Modelling habit formation in the real world. <em>European Journal of Social Psychology</em>, 40, 998–1009.'],decisions:['Tversky, A., & Kahneman, D. (1974). Judgment under uncertainty: Heuristics and biases. <em>Science</em>, 185, 1124–1131.'],biases:['Pronin, E., Lin, D. Y., & Ross, L. (2002). The bias blind spot: Perceptions of bias in self versus others. <em>Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin</em>, 28, 369–381.']};const topicPage=t=>`<article class="art"><p class="eyebrow"><a href="#/minds">Understanding Minds</a> / ${t.c}</p><h1>${t.t}</h1><p><em>${t.s}</em></p><h2>Key points</h2><ul>${t.k.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join('')}</ul><h2>Myth and evidence</h2><div class="mf"><div class="card"><span class="tag">Myth</span><p>${t.m}</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag d">Evidence</span><p>${t.f}</p></div></div>${t.c=='Everyday'?'':'<h2>Getting help</h2><p>If this sounds familiar, only a qualified professional can say whether it applies. Start with a doctor or psychologist. To support someone, see <a href="#/comfort">Be Someone’s Comfort</a>, and for services see <a href="#/paws">Helping Paws</a>.</p>'}<h2>Sources and further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="${t.c=='Everyday'?'https://www.apa.org/topics':NIMH+t.u}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${t.c=='Everyday'?'American Psychological Association: Topics':'NIMH: health topic page'}</a></li>${(EX[t.id]||[]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join('')}</ul><p class="note">An overview-level summary for general education. The linked source has the full detail.</p>${dis}</article>`;
const G=[['Anxiety','Ask what helps rather than saying there’s nothing to worry about. Avoid pushing them into feared situations suddenly. Offer practical help, like finding a professional.'],['Depression','Keep in touch even if they withdraw, and offer small practical help. Take talk of hopelessness or self-harm seriously and involve a trusted adult or emergency services if there is danger.'],['OCD','Be patient and avoid mocking rituals. Ask their clinician how to respond to rituals and reassurance-seeking, since accommodating them can keep the cycle going.'],['Panic','During an attack, stay calm, speak slowly and ask what helps. Don’t tell them to calm down. If it is the first attack, encourage a medical check.'],['Trauma','Let them decide what to share and don’t press for details. Respect triggers and encourage support from trauma-informed professionals.'],['Eating disorders','Focus on the person rather than food, weight or appearance, and avoid comments about bodies. Encourage seeing a doctor early, and involve a trusted adult if you are a teenager.'],['Difficult times without a diagnosis','Nobody needs a label to deserve support. Check in regularly, listen, and encourage professional help if things persist or worsen.']];
const ARTS=[{id:'songs',c:'Everyday Questions',t:'Why do songs get stuck in our heads?',s:'Involuntary musical imagery, or “earworms”, is common. Here’s what researchers know.',b:['Songs that loop in your mind are called earworms, or involuntary musical imagery. Surveys suggest most people experience them regularly.','They tend to be triggered by recent listening, memory cues, or moments when the mind is idle or tired, and they often involve simple, repetitive melodies.','They usually fade on their own. Many people find that absorbing tasks help, though evidence for any single fix is limited.']},{id:'procrastinate',c:'Everyday Questions',t:'Why do we procrastinate?',s:'Research increasingly treats delay as a problem of managing emotions, not time.',b:['Procrastination is voluntarily delaying a task despite expecting to be worse off for it. Research increasingly treats it as a problem of managing emotions rather than managing time.','People often delay tasks that trigger boredom, anxiety or self-doubt, because avoiding the task brings short-term relief. The cost arrives later.','Strategies with some support include breaking tasks into small first steps, reducing friction and being kind to yourself about past delays. Persistent, distressing procrastination can be worth discussing with a professional.']},{id:'memories',c:'Everyday Questions',t:'Why do memories change?',s:'Remembering is a reconstruction, not a playback.',b:['Memory isn’t a recording. Remembering is a reconstruction built from stored fragments and shaped by what you know and expect when you recall.','In classic eyewitness studies by Elizabeth Loftus and colleagues, changing the wording of a question altered people’s estimates and later reports of an event.','That doesn’t make memories useless. Most core memories are reasonably reliable, but vividness and confidence are not proof of accuracy.']},{id:'dreams',c:'Everyday Questions',t:'Why do we dream?',s:'There is no single accepted answer yet.',b:['There is no single accepted answer. Dreams occur most vividly in REM sleep but can happen in other sleep stages too.','Proposed explanations include memory processing, emotional regulation, and a by-product of brain activity during sleep. Each has some support, and none is settled.','Claims that dreams have fixed symbolic meanings aren’t supported by good evidence.']}];
