R['/topic/anxiety']=()=>`<article class="art"><p class="eyebrow"><a href="#/minds">Understanding Minds</a> / Anxiety</p><h1>Understanding Anxiety</h1><p><em>Anxiety is a normal human response, but persistent anxiety can sometimes interfere with everyday life.</em></p>
<h2>What is anxiety?</h2><p>Anxiety is the body and mind’s response to perceived threat or uncertainty. It involves thoughts (worry, expecting the worst), emotions (fear, unease), physical responses (a racing heart, tension, restlessness) and behaviour (avoiding situations, seeking reassurance).</p>
<h2>Everyday anxiety versus anxiety disorders</h2><p>Feeling anxious before an exam or a difficult conversation is part of ordinary life. Anxiety disorders involve a persistent pattern of symptoms that causes significant distress or interferes with school, work, relationships or daily functioning. One symptom on its own does not mean someone has a disorder.</p>
<h2>Myths and facts</h2><div class="mf"><div class="card"><span class="tag">Myth</span><p>Anxiety is simply worrying too much.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag d">Fact</span><p>It involves patterns of emotional, physical and behavioural responses, and can affect functioning.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag">Myth</span><p>People with anxiety are just seeking attention.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag d">Fact</span><p>Anxiety disorders are recognised health conditions, and many people hide their struggles.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag">Myth</span><p>Anxiety can’t be treated.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="tag d">Fact</span><p>Effective treatments exist, including psychotherapy and, for some people, medication.</p></div></div>
<h2>When it may be worth professional assessment</h2><p>Consider speaking with a doctor, psychologist or other qualified professional if anxiety is persistent, hard to control, or getting in the way of daily life. Diagnosis requires an appropriate assessment, not a checklist.</p>
<h2>Evidence-based approaches</h2><p>Major health bodies describe psychotherapy (especially cognitive behavioural therapy) and medication as established options, and the right choice depends on the person and the condition. A clinician can discuss this.</p>
<h2>Supporting someone with anxiety</h2><p>See <a href="#/comfort">Be Someone’s Comfort</a>.</p>
<h2>Sources and further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/anxiety-disorders" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIMH: Anxiety Disorders</a></li><li><a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/anxiety-disorders" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO: Anxiety disorders fact sheet</a></li></ul>${dis}</article>`;
R['/comfort']=()=>hero('Supporting others','Be Someone’s Comfort','Learn how to listen, offer support, respect boundaries, and help someone find appropriate care.')+`<div class="art"><h2>What you can do</h2><ul><li>Listen without rushing to solve the problem.</li><li>Ask what kind of support would help.</li><li>Avoid assumptions about their experience.</li><li>Respect boundaries and personal choices.</li><li>Offer practical help, such as company at an appointment.</li><li>Encourage professional support when needed.</li><li>Involve a trusted adult or professional if safety is a concern.</li></ul>
<h2>What to avoid saying</h2><div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Instead of</th><th>You could try</th></tr><tr><td>“Just relax.”</td><td>“That sounds really hard. What’s it like right now?”</td></tr><tr><td>“Everyone feels like that.”</td><td>“I’m glad you told me. I’d like to understand.”</td></tr><tr><td>“You have nothing to worry about.”</td><td>“Is there anything that would help right now?”</td></tr><tr><td>“Why don’t you just stop thinking about it?”</td><td>“Do you want to talk, or would a distraction help?”</td></tr></table></div><p>No single sentence fixes things. Being steady and willing to listen matters more than perfect wording.</p>
<h2>Looking after yourself</h2><p>You are not responsible for acting as a therapist or managing someone else’s treatment. Set realistic limits, and talk to a trusted adult or professional when a situation feels beyond you.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>If someone may be in immediate danger</strong>, contact local emergency services (112 in India) and involve a trusted adult. Don’t handle an emergency alone. See <a href="#/paws">Helping Paws</a>.</div>
