R['/article/ten-percent']=()=>`<article class="art"><p class="eyebrow"><a href="#/journal">Cat’s Journal</a> / Myth Check</p><h1>Do we only use 10% of our brains?</h1><p><em>Verdict: myth.</em></p><p>The claim is repeated in films and advertising, but brain imaging shows activity across virtually all brain regions over the course of a day, even during sleep. Different tasks engage different networks, which is not the same as most of the brain sitting idle.</p><p>The idea also conflicts with what we see in injury: damage to almost any part of the brain tends to have some effect. And the brain uses a large share of the body’s energy, which would be hard to explain if 90% were unused.</p><p><strong>What this doesn’t mean:</strong> it doesn’t say anything about “unlocking” abilities. Learning changes the brain, but through practice rather than by switching on unused tissue.</p><h2>References</h2><ul><li>Beyerstein, B. L. (1999). Whence cometh the myth that we only use 10% of our brains? In S. Della Sala (Ed.), <em>Mind Myths</em>. Wiley.</li><li>Lilienfeld, S. O., Lynn, S. J., Ruscio, J., & Beyerstein, B. L. (2010). <em>50 Great Myths of Popular Psychology</em>. Wiley-Blackwell.</li></ul></article>`;
R['/bella']=()=>hero('Meet Bella','Meet Bella','Bella was my first cat, a golden white Persian, and the inspiration behind Comfort Cat.',1)+`<figure class="art" style="margin:0 0 10px"><img class="pic" style="max-width:340px" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Photograph of Bella as a kitten: a fluffy ginger and cream cat sitting upright and looking at the camera"><figcaption>The real Bella.</figcaption></figure><div class="art"><h2>Why Comfort Cat exists</h2><p>To make psychology easier to understand and to help people find reliable information. Bella gives the project a recognisable face; the content itself stays grounded in research.</p><h2>Our mission</h2><p>Make psychology easier to understand, challenge misinformation, and help people navigate mental-health information responsibly.</p><h2>What Comfort Cat believes</h2><ul><li>Psychology should be understandable.</li><li>Evidence matters.</li><li>Mental-health information should be responsible.</li><li>People are not their diagnoses.</li><li>Learning about symptoms is not diagnosing yourself.</li><li>Supporting someone doesn’t replace professional care.</li></ul><h2>What Comfort Cat is not</h2><p>It does not provide individual diagnosis, psychotherapy, emergency intervention, or personalized medical advice.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p>For questions, corrections or accessibility feedback, a contact address will be added here soon.</p></div>`;
